 ### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-*   **Noise Model:** Raw cryo-ET data follows a Poisson noise model, but preprocessing (motion/intensity correction) makes it approximately Gaussian, which is assumed for the loss function [S1].
-*   **Discontinuities:** Independent optimization of octree nodes causes discontinuity artifacts at grid edges. This is mitigated by the boundary consistency loss $L_{bc}$ [S1].
-*   **Fiducial Markers:** Rays intersecting fiducial markers or padded gray regions interfere with reconstruction. A binary mask $M$ excludes these rays from the data-fidelity loss [S1].
-*   **Standard Neural Fields:** MLP-based neural fields are inadequate for high-noise cryo-ET as they either learn noise or oversmooth features. ADG addresses this via explicit regularization and adaptive grids [S1].
+*   **High Noise:** Standard neural fields fail on cryo-ET data because they learn noise or oversmooth. ADG handles this via regularization (TV, CNLC, BC) [S1].
+*   **Discontinuity Artifacts:** Independent optimization of octree nodes causes discontinuities at grid edges. This is mitigated by the Boundary Consistency Loss ($L_{bc}$) [S1].
+*   **Fiducial Markers and Borders:** Fiducial markers and padded gray regions interfere with reconstruction. A binary mask $M$ is applied to the data-fidelity loss to exclude these rays [S1].
+*   **Noise Model:** Raw cryo-ET data follows a Poisson noise model, but after preprocessing (motion/intensity correction), it is approximated as Gaussian noise for simplicity [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
 
-*   **Time Complexity:** Faster than MLP-based neural fields because density query uses trilinear interpolation ($O(1)$) instead of network evaluation ($O(D)$ where $D$ is network depth/width) [S1]. However, octree updates involve solving a mixed-integer program, which adds overhead [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Lower than classical tomographic reconstruction techniques as it does not store the entire 3D volume during optimization, only the octree nodes and their grids [S1]. Memory usage scales with the number of enabled nodes and grid resolution $N_x \times N_y \times N_z$ [S1].
-*   **Performance:** Demonstrated on EMPIAR 10643, EMPIAR 10453, and EMPIAR 11462 datasets [S1]. Outperforms SART+TV, Kniesel et al., and NeAT in handling high noise and preserving details [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Fast querying, better detail preservation in complex regions, effective denoising of uniform regions, explicit regularization capabilities [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires careful tuning of regularization weights ($k_{tv}, k_{cnlc}, k_{bc}$) and octree update parameters. Mixed-integer programming for node updates can be computationally expensive [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Faster than MLP-based neural fields because querying density uses trilinear interpolation ($O(1)$) instead of evaluating a deep network [S1]. The octree structure accelerates optimization by focusing computations on regions with features [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Lower than classical tomographic reconstruction techniques because it does not store the entire 3D volume during optimization; it only stores the octree nodes and their density grids [S1]. The memory usage scales with the number of active octree nodes, which is kept below a fixed limit [S1].
+*   **Measured Performance:** Evaluated on EMPIAR 10643, EMPIAR 10453, and EMPIAR 11462 datasets [S1]. Outperforms SART+TV, Kniesel et al., and NeAT in reconstruction quality and denoising [S1].
+*   **Strengths:**
+    *   Better detail preservation in complex regions and denoising in uniform regions due to adaptive octree [S1].
+    *   Faster querying and optimization compared to MLPs [S1].
+    *   Flexible regularization framework for high-noise data [S1].
+*   **Limitations:**
+    *   Requires careful tuning of hyperparameters ($k_{tv}, k_{cnlc}, k_{bc}, N_{max}$) [S1].
+    *   The mixed-integer program for octree update adds computational overhead during the coarse phase [S1].
+    *   Discontinuity artifacts can still occur if boundary consistency is not properly weighted [S1].
+*   **When to Use:** Cryo-ET reconstruction and denoising where high noise and missing wedge artifacts are present [S1].
+*   **When Not to Use:** Scenes with very low feature density where a uniform grid might be more efficient, or when real-time rendering is required without pre-computation (though ADG is faster than MLPs, it is still slower than direct grid lookup) [S1].
 
 ## Background
 
-*   **Origin:** Proposed by Wang et al. in "Adaptive differentiable grids for cryo-electron tomography reconstruction and denoising" (2023) [S1].
-*   **History:** Builds on recent advances in neural representations (NeRF) and octree-based structures (ACORN, NeAT) to address the specific challenges of cryo-ET, such as low SNR and missing wedge artifacts [S1].
-*   **Usage:** Applied to cryo-electron tomography reconstruction and denoising for biological samples like viruses and cells [S1].
